WEBVTT

00:00.370 --> 00:02.420
Here's a quick one to start.

00:02.420 --> 00:04.570
Look at the water graph for 2024.

00:04.970 --> 00:09.520
Northport, 180 litres. Westvale, 45.

00:09.670 --> 00:12.720
How many times higher is Northport?

00:12.870 --> 00:15.520
Pause the video and work it out.

00:16.920 --> 00:17.720
Ready?

00:17.970 --> 00:21.720
180 divided by 45 is 4.

00:21.720 --> 00:25.820
So Northport's water use was four times as high as Westvale's.

00:26.570 --> 00:28.420
And you can turn it around.

00:28.420 --> 00:32.120
Westvale's figure was a quarter of Northport's.

00:32.270 --> 00:35.720
Notice it's still like with like:

00:35.720 --> 00:38.820
Northport's water use, Westvale's.
A figure against a figure.

00:39.270 --> 00:45.120
Now 2004. Northport, 150. Westvale, 60.

00:45.570 --> 00:48.320
Pause again. Is it twice as high?

00:48.570 --> 00:51.720
It isn't. 150 divided by 60 is 2.5.

00:51.720 --> 00:55.270
So in 2004,

00:55.270 --> 01:00.820
Northport used two and a half times
as much water as Westvale.

01:00.970 --> 01:04.120
“Twice” would be a small mistake,

01:04.120 --> 01:07.120
but it's an inaccurate figure,
and accuracy matters in Task 1.

01:07.270 --> 01:08.820
Last one.

01:08.970 --> 01:16.120
Easton in 2014 was 120, and Westvale was 50.

01:16.270 --> 01:19.120
That's 2.4. Not a neat number.

01:19.270 --> 01:22.120
So don't force it.

01:22.120 --> 01:25.320
Say “almost two and a half times as high”.

01:25.320 --> 01:29.720
Use words like roughly, almost and just under
when the division isn't neat.

01:29.870 --> 01:33.320
Now try the questions on the right page.
